-- chat_cases_activate (Phase 12.4 plan 02, CASE-03, CASE-04, CASE-21; D-801, D-802, D-814, D-838) — THE ONE-WAY STEP.
-- Generated by drizzle-kit for the index and the constraint, then EXTENDED BY HAND with the lock, the backfill call
-- and the validation, and put in this order.
--
-- ── WHY ONE FILE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- There must be no committed state in which a row is numbered before the old rows are (the backfill numbers every
-- legacy conversation by first message instant BEFORE any case born later could take a number), no state in which a
-- support row can still be stored without a state, and no state in which BOTH unique indexes exist (the old one would
-- refuse the second case of a member that the new one allows). So everything below commits together or not at all.
-- ALL FIVE STEPS ARE ONE `do` BLOCK, which is ONE statement and therefore ONE transaction under every runner: the
-- ledgered fan-out runner wraps a file in a transaction, but the local Supabase CLI sends the statements of a file one
-- by one (probed: a bare `lock table` fails there with 25P01), and an explicit `begin`/`commit` would clash with the
-- runner's own. The first step keeps writers out of the table until the block commits.
--
-- ── WHAT IT DOES, IN ORDER ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--   1. `lock table … share row exclusive`: concurrent inserts and updates of conversations wait; plain reads do not.
--      (The message trigger updates the conversation row, so a message in flight waits too, for a few milliseconds.)
--   2. `app.chat_cases_backfill()` (defined in `*_chat_cases_transitions.sql`, which sorts BEFORE this file): every
--      support conversation that has no state becomes a numbered case, open or closed (D-814), with its history rows
--      and NO notification. A conversation with a state is left alone, so this also holds on a project that already
--      has cases born after plan 01.
--   3. `chat_conversations_support_state_chk` (a support row has a state), added NOT VALID and then validated: it is
--      added only now because a NOT VALID constraint is still evaluated on the new version of every updated row, and
--      it would have failed every message on a conversation that predates the backfill (D-836).
--   4. the new unique index `chat_conversations_one_active_case_per_member` (active = open, in_progress or resolved).
--   5. the DROP of `chat_conversations_one_support_per_member`.
--
-- The expand-only grep over the migrations prints exactly ONE hit in this file, the DROP of the old index at the end of the block, and it is the
-- allowed one: the old index guaranteed one conversation per member, which is exactly what D-801 gives up. Releasing
-- this file takes the PREVIOUS API revision's member send down (its `on conflict … where kind = 'support'` no longer
-- matches any index: SQLSTATE 42P10) until the new API serves; plan 18 names the release order.

do $activation$
begin
  lock table public.chat_conversations in share row exclusive mode;
  perform app.chat_cases_backfill();
  alter table public.chat_conversations
    add constraint chat_conversations_support_state_chk
    check (kind <> 'support' or state is not null) not valid;
  alter table public.chat_conversations validate constraint chat_conversations_support_state_chk;
  create unique index chat_conversations_one_active_case_per_member
    on public.chat_conversations using btree (tenant_id, created_by_user_id)
    where kind = 'support' and state in ('open','in_progress','resolved');
  drop index public.chat_conversations_one_support_per_member;
end
$activation$;
